    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5807",
      "problem": "Graph the curve corresponding to the equation $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(3-x)(x+1)^2.$",
      "worked_solution": "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(3-0)(0+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(3)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-1.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*. If we were to expand the parentheses, we'd get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(3-x)(x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238854/assets/14c1e6701c5f52f37788d22a5e09ba0bc92897ea64fa64ce0f3717115d9604af.png)",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238854/observations/step-e5807-65634a750ddacf5eea98.html",
